/* Independent scenario-based mastery concepts: 4 new questions per lesson. */
window.CHESS_ACADEMY_MASTERY_CONCEPTS=[{"id":"M01-Q1","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A black bishop attacks your rook, and it is your turn. What should you check first?","options":["Whether the rook can be captured if you do nothing","Whether you can name the opening","Whether to offer a draw","Whether your rook looks active"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Check the opponent's immediate threat before following your own plan.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M01-Q2","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You want to attack a pawn, but your queen is hanging. What is the safer thought process?","options":["Ignore the queen and attack","Look for a way to save the queen or meet the attack","Move a random pawn","Offer a queen trade without calculating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"First identify your opponent's threat to the queen; only then examine safe candidate moves.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M01-Q3","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After choosing a promising move, what question helps catch blunders?","options":["How many moves have passed?","Will my opponent resign?","What is the strongest reply my opponent can make?","How flashy does my move look?"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Looking at the opponent's strongest response is the final blunder check.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M01-Q4","lesson":1,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent checks your king. Which moves should you examine before playing a quiet move?","options":["Only moves that attack their queen","Only pawn moves","Only knight moves","Legal ways to escape, block or capture the checking piece"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Every response to check must leave your king safe; most quiet moves are illegal.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M02-Q1","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight checks the king and attacks a rook at the same time. What makes this a fork?","options":["The knight attacks two targets with one move","The knight moves along a straight line","The rook cannot ever move","Both targets must be pawns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A fork occurs when one piece attacks at least two enemy targets simultaneously.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M02-Q2","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your knight could fork a king and queen, but the landing square is defended by a pawn. What must you examine?","options":["Whether the knight can be captured immediately","Whether the board has an open file","Whether the queen can castle","Whether your king is near the center"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A visually attractive fork may fail if the knight is simply captured; calculate legal responses.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M02-Q3","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which knight feature makes forks easy to overlook?","options":["It can castle","It attacks in an L shape and can leap over pieces","It moves diagonally like a bishop","It may capture its own pawn"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The knight's unusual jump allows simultaneous attacks from unexpected squares.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M02-Q4","lesson":2,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight attacks both enemy rooks, but one rook can capture the knight. What conclusion is safest?","options":["The fork guarantees a win","The knight's attack counts as checkmate","You must calculate whether the fork works","The two rooks become pinned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Counting targets alone is insufficient; an attack is only useful when the opponent cannot refute it.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M03-Q1","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop aims at your queen through your knight. What may happen if you move the knight?","options":["The bishop may attack or capture the queen","Moving the knight is always illegal","The queen becomes a pawn","The bishop must retreat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is a relative pin: moving the front piece may expose a more valuable piece.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M03-Q2","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is special about an absolute pin?","options":["A rook is on the board","Moving the pinned piece would expose its own king to check","The pinned piece is always a bishop","The pinned piece cannot be captured"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A piece may not legally move if doing so exposes its king to an attack.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M03-Q3","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook checks a king, with a queen lined up behind that king. What tactic can follow?","options":["En passant","Castling","Skewer","Stalemate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A skewer attacks the valuable front piece and then takes something behind it.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M03-Q4","lesson":3,"kind":"mc","prompt":"One bishop move uncovers a rook check and also gives a bishop check. What is this called?","options":["An isolated pawn","An opposition","A fork by a pawn","Double check"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Two separate pieces give check simultaneously, forcing a king move.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M04-Q1","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your king has castled behind pawns, but an enemy rook can reach your back rank. What should you inspect?","options":["Whether your king has an escape square","Whether your knight is developed","Whether your rook is on an open file","Whether a pawn reached the center"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A king with no escape can be vulnerable to a rook or queen check on its home rank.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M04-Q2","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can a queen-and-bishop attack on the king be stronger than a queen alone?","options":["The queen becomes worth extra points","Both pieces can cover escape squares and create mating threats","The bishop can jump pieces","The king may no longer capture"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mating patterns depend on coordinated attackers controlling escape and defensive squares.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M04-Q3","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An enemy queen gives check, but your king has a safe square. Is this automatically checkmate?","options":["Yes, all queen checks are mate","Yes, if the queen is near your king","No, checkmate requires no legal escape or defense","No, because queens cannot check alone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checkmate requires the king to be in check with no legal move, block, or capture to stop it.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M04-Q4","lesson":4,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When checking whether a position is mate, which question matters most?","options":["Did you capture a rook?","Is your queen active?","Did you move the same piece twice?","Can the defending side legally get out of check?"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A check is mate only when all legal defenses fail.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M05-Q1","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White plays e4 and develops a knight to f3. What should White usually aim to do next?","options":["Bring other pieces into useful play while watching threats","Move the queen out alone to attack pawns","Return the knight to its original square without reason","Move every edge pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opening play typically develops pieces, influences the center, and prepares king safety.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M05-Q2","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your bishop is developed, but an enemy pawn attacks it. Is moving it again necessarily a mistake?","options":["Yes, always","No, avoiding a real threat can justify another move","Yes, bishops must move only once","No, because opening rules never apply"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Opening principles are guidelines that yield to concrete tactical threats.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M05-Q3","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a common practical purpose of castling early?","options":["Immediately promote a pawn","Guarantee a draw","Move the king toward safety and activate a rook","Remove every central pawn"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Castling generally improves king safety and connects a rook to useful central files.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M05-Q4","lesson":5,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is copying an opening line without checking threats dangerous?","options":["Because every opening has only four moves","Because knights move differently by opening","Because pawn captures are optional","Because your opponent may play a move that changes what is safe"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Always evaluate the actual position rather than following memorized moves blindly.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M06-Q1","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After ten opening moves, White has developed four pieces and Black only one. What should Black notice?","options":["Development is lagging, so threats and piece activity matter","Black automatically wins","The game is a stalemate","White's pieces must retreat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Count active pieces, king safety, and central control when evaluating an opening.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M06-Q2","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent plays an unexpected move. What is a sound next step?","options":["Replay your memorized line anyway","Identify its threat and choose a safe, purposeful response","Trade queens without looking","Move your king to the center"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unfamiliar moves should be assessed on their concrete merits, not ignored.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M06-Q3","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A player has castled but left a knight en prise (able to be captured for free). What does this demonstrate?","options":["Castling is illegal","Development is always irrelevant","A good principle cannot compensate for a tactical blunder","A knight can promote"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Opening principles are useful, but concrete tactical losses must be checked first.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M06-Q4","lesson":6,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a useful opening diagnosis rather than a name?","options":["How many moves looked impressive","Whether the pieces match a video","Who played the newest novelty","Which king is safer and whose pieces are more active"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Position-based comparisons are more reliable than memorized opening labels.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M07-Q1","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Italian Game, the bishop on c4 usually puts pressure on which Black square?","options":["f7","a7","h6","b8"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The bishop on c4 aims at f7, often a sensitive square near Black's king.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M07-Q2","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White plays c3 in a quiet Italian position. What central pawn push is often being prepared?","options":["a4","d4","h4","f4"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The c3 pawn supports White's d4 advance in the center.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M07-Q3","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After ...Bc5 and Bc4, what is a sound plan before opening the center?","options":["Ignore king safety","Move the queen six times","Develop, secure the king, and prepare pawn breaks","Move every pawn on the kingside"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quiet Italian positions often reward patient development and coordinated central play.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M07-Q4","lesson":7,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop aims toward f7 but cannot capture safely. What should you do?","options":["Sacrifice it regardless","Assume the attack wins","Give up the center","Calculate defenders and develop rather than attacking blindly"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pressure on f7 is a feature, not an automatic tactical win.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M08-Q1","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After White plays c3 in an Italian position, what does a later d4 often challenge?","options":["Black's e5 pawn and central control","Black's a7 pawn","White's king on e1","White's rook on a1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"d4 is a central pawn break that puts pressure on Black's e5 center.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M08-Q2","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Black can capture the pawn after d4. What should White do before pushing?","options":["Trust the opening name","Calculate captures, recaptures, and discovered attacks","Always trade queens","Retreat every bishop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pawn break changes open lines and material balance, requiring concrete calculation.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M08-Q3","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A central exchange opens a diagonal onto your queen. What risk might arise?","options":["Your bishop gets promoted","Your king can move twice","A tactic on the newly opened diagonal","Your pawn becomes a knight"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Opening lines can expose valuable pieces to discovered attacks.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M08-Q4","lesson":8,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When does a central pawn break make sense?","options":["Whenever you're bored","Only on move four","Only after both queens move","When the resulting exchanges and piece activity are favorable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pawn breaks should be judged by the actual resulting position, not played automatically.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M09-Q1","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In a Two Knights Defense position, Black attacks White's e4 pawn. What must White assess?","options":["Whether the pawn needs defending or tactical compensation exists","Whether Black's king can promote","Whether rooks jump","Whether to move an a-pawn regardless"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The immediate e4 threat may require defense or a calculated tactical response.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M09-Q2","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A White bishop looks at f7. Why might an early sacrifice still fail?","options":["Because bishops cannot capture pawns","Defenders and king escapes may refute the attack","Because f7 is never weak","Because a bishop is worth nine points"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An attractive target is not enough; examine the defender's responses.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M09-Q3","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Black develops a knight to f6 while White's king remains in the center. Which concern should White have?","options":["The knight makes castling illegal forever","White wins a queen instantly","Central threats and time needed to castle","Knight moves can be ignored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Development can create threats; safely completing king development remains important.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M09-Q4","lesson":9,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is a calm Italian setup sometimes preferable to an immediate attack?","options":["Because captures are illegal","Because the bishop must retreat","Because a queen cannot attack alone","It allows development and king safety before forcing tactics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Quiet variations can build a healthy position without speculative sacrifices.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M10-Q1","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Caro-Kann, what is Black's main idea behind 1...c6?","options":["Support a future ...d5 challenge to White's center","Attack h2 immediately","Castle on the first move","Promote the c-pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c6 reinforces the d5 square and supports the central counterattack.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M10-Q2","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why is developing the light-squared bishop before ...e6 often useful?","options":["It prevents every fork","...e6 would otherwise limit the bishop's diagonal","It captures White's queen","The bishop could never move later"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Activating the bishop before blocking it with a pawn is an important Caro-Kann theme.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M10-Q3","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White's center looks large, but Black's ...d5 pawn challenges it. What should Black evaluate?","options":["Only the name Caro-Kann","Whether the board is symmetrical","Captures and central pawn tension","Only pawn promotions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Central challenges work when the ensuing exchanges support your pieces and structure.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M10-Q4","lesson":10,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your bishop attacks a knight but White can chase it with pawns. What matters?","options":["The bishop must sacrifice itself","All retreats are losing","Pawn pushes are always strongest","A safe retreat and the pawn weaknesses created"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Calculate how to keep the bishop useful if White gains space by attacking it.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M11-Q1","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White establishes a pawn on e5 in the Advance Caro-Kann. What has changed?","options":["White has gained space, and Black may target the pawn chain","Black has already lost","Both sides must castle queenside","White's pawns cannot move"],"answer":0,"explanation":"White gains space; Black can seek pawn breaks that challenge the structure.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M11-Q2","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Black's light-squared bishop is still on c8. Why might ...Bf5 be attractive?","options":["It automatically wins a rook","It develops before the e-pawn blocks the diagonal","It guarantees checkmate","It traps the bishop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"...Bf5 is a common way to activate that bishop in the Advance variation.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M11-Q3","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A pawn chain has a base and an advanced point. Where should you often look for a counterattack?","options":["At your king's own pawn shield","At the move number","At a pawn break targeting the chain's support","At an unrelated a-pawn"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pawn breaks against the base of a chain can undermine its most advanced pawn.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M11-Q4","lesson":11,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What would make an Exchange Caro-Kann position different from the Advance?","options":["White still has a pawn on e5","Black must keep the c-pawn","Neither side can trade","White has exchanged its e-pawn on d5 instead of advancing it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Exchange and Advance structures lead to different central pawns and plans.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M12-Q1","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Queen's Gambit, why does White usually play c4 after d4 and ...d5?","options":["Pressure Black's central d5 pawn","Give away the queen","Checkmate at once","Prevent all exchanges"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The c-pawn challenges Black's d5 pawn and central control.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M12-Q2","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Queen's Gambit Declined, Black plays ...e6. What trade-off may follow?","options":["The king must stay on e8","The pawn supports d5 but can restrict the c8 bishop","The d5 pawn is undefended","Black may castle illegally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"...e6 reinforces d5 but reduces the bishop's freedom until Black finds a plan.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M12-Q3","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White develops Bg5 and pins a knight on f6. What is important to calculate?","options":["Whether the bishop can become a pawn","How to promote the knight","Whether the pin creates real pressure or can be broken","Whether all pawns should advance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pins have value only if the tactical and positional consequences support them.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M12-Q4","lesson":12,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Black hopes to challenge White's d4 center. Which approach fits a QGD plan?","options":["Ignore every central pawn","Push the h-pawn to h5 automatically","Sacrifice the queen","Prepare an appropriate pawn break such as ...c5"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The ...c5 break is a common way to contest White's central d4 pawn.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M13-Q1","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You reach a middlegame with no immediate tactics. What is a reasonable first step?","options":["Identify weak squares, threats, and your least active piece","Move randomly until something happens","Trade every piece automatically","Advance the king into attack"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plans should grow from position-specific weaknesses and useful improvements.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M13-Q2","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight has no useful squares while your other pieces are active. What could your plan prioritize?","options":["Abandoning the knight","Finding a better route for that knight","Moving the queen repeatedly","Creating an unnecessary weakness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Improving your worst-placed piece is a concrete, practical middlegame plan.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M13-Q3","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent changes the pawn structure. What should happen to your plan?","options":["Nothing can change it","You must resign","Reassess open lines and new weaknesses","Keep the old plan without checking"],"answer":2,"explanation":"New pawn structures alter squares and files, so plans need reevaluation.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M13-Q4","lesson":13,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You see a weak enemy pawn but your own king is under attack. What matters first?","options":["Capture the pawn regardless","Ask how to win on time","Choose the flashiest pawn move","Handle immediate king safety before pursuing long-term targets"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An urgent danger overrides slow strategic goals.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M14-Q1","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When choosing between two moves, which reply should you test for each?","options":["The opponent's strongest plausible response","The opponent's weakest response","Only a move you want them to play","No response at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calculation requires considering the opponent's best resistance.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M14-Q2","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook check forces the king to move, exposing a queen behind it. What tactic is occurring?","options":["A pawn race","A skewer","An isolated pawn","A stalemate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A skewer forces the king away and exposes the more vulnerable piece behind.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M14-Q3","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You calculate one line deeply but miss a simple capture. What step would have helped?","options":["Ignoring all checks","Choosing a move by appearance","Scanning checks, captures and threats before deciding","Only counting your own pawns"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A systematic forcing-move scan catches tactics missed by a single preferred line.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M14-Q4","lesson":14,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your candidate wins a pawn but allows forced mate against you. How should you evaluate it?","options":["It must be best because it wins material","It is equal because pawns are worth one","It is best if it gives check","Reject it if the opponent has a forced mating response"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Opponent's strongest forcing replies matter more than material gains.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M15-Q1","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Two kings face each other with one square between them, and it is Black's move. Which idea may help White?","options":["Black may need to give way if no useful pawn move exists","White can move twice","Black's king is already checkmated","Opposition automatically wins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Having opposition can force a king to yield when the defender has no useful waiting move.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M15-Q2","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does opposition not automatically mean a win?","options":["Because kings can stand adjacent","Pawns, turn order and other legal moves still matter","Because a king must always remain on its starting square","Because pawns cannot promote"],"answer":1,"explanation":"King opposition is a tool, not a complete result for every pawn ending.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M15-Q3","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A pawn is three moves from promotion and an opposing king is far away. What should you compare?","options":["Only pawn color","Only whether a rook is on board","The king's route and both sides' move order","The name of the opening"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Count actual king and pawn moves to see whether the pawn can be stopped.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M15-Q4","lesson":15,"kind":"mc","prompt":"The king can catch a passed pawn if it is given enough turns. What shortcut might help?","options":["A knight fork","A discovered attack","A back-rank pattern","The square of the pawn"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The square rule estimates whether a defending king can intercept a passed pawn.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M16-Q1","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After a loss, what should you record before asking Stockfish for help?","options":["What you intended and which threats you noticed","Just the final score","Only the opponent's username","Only the opening name"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Your own decisions and reasoning are the most valuable source for improvement.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M16-Q2","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"The engine prefers a move you never considered. What should you ask?","options":["Whether the engine is cheating","Which threats or resources make it stronger","Whether the time control is wrong","Whether the board has enough pieces"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A useful review explains why the alternative works and what your thinking missed.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M16-Q3","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You found a tactical mistake. What makes a useful study note?","options":["The engine's number alone","A claim you are bad at chess","A repeatable check that would catch the error next time","Only the move number"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Turn a mistake into an actionable habit, such as checking opponent captures.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M16-Q4","lesson":16,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You want to improve from one game. What review order is most educational?","options":["See engine scores before any thought","Memorize every line","Replay only winning moves","Analyze your reasoning first, then compare it with the engine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Independent reflection helps you learn the decision process rather than copying moves.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M17-Q1","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An enemy pawn attacks your knight and the knight has no defender. What is the immediate concern?","options":["The pawn may win the knight on its next move","The knight automatically gives check","Your king can castle twice","You must promote a pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An unprotected or loose piece requires attention when attacked.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M17-Q2","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A bishop is attacked twice and defended once. Can counting alone prove it loses?","options":["Yes, always","Not always; piece values and move order also matter","Yes, only bishops count","No, because bishops cannot be captured"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Exchanges depend on attackers' order, values and tactics, not just raw counts.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M17-Q3","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are about to move a rook away from protecting a knight. What should you check?","options":["Which opening is named for the move","Whether the opponent can castle","Whether the knight becomes undefended and vulnerable","Whether the rook looks more attractive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Moving a defender can leave another piece hanging.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M17-Q4","lesson":17,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does 'loose piece' usually mean?","options":["A piece worth zero points","A piece that cannot legally move","A piece that has changed color","A piece without adequate protection"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Loose or undefended pieces often become tactical targets.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M18-Q1","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You have two candidate moves, one aggressive and one defensive. Which is the best first evaluation?","options":["Compare their concrete replies, not just their appearance","Always play the aggressive one","Always play the defensive one","Move whichever piece is closest"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even appealing plans must be checked against the opponent's strongest responses.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M18-Q2","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your first choice allows a check on your king. What should you do?","options":["Ignore it if your move attacks a pawn","Calculate the check and look for safer alternatives","Move your queen randomly","Assume the check is harmless"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Counterchecks can overturn an apparently good move.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M18-Q3","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a candidate move?","options":["A move only from an opening book","Any move that gives check","A serious possible move to examine before choosing","A move Stockfish must approve"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Candidate moves are plausible options compared during calculation.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M18-Q4","lesson":18,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the final safeguard before committing to a chosen move?","options":["Count the total moves played","Ask whether the opponent knows theory","Check the clock's color","Look once more for opponent checks and captures"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A final blunder scan can catch immediate tactical refutations.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M19-Q1","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"If your king is in check, what must your next move accomplish?","options":["Leave your king on a square that is not attacked","Win a pawn on another wing","Develop a bishop regardless","Offer a draw immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Legal moves in check must remove the check.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M19-Q2","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A queen attacks your queen. Which response can sometimes be valid?","options":["Ignore the attack without calculation","Capture the attacker if legal and safe","Move an unrelated pawn with no threat","Move your king into check"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You may retreat, defend, block or capture an attacker, depending on the position.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M19-Q3","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent threatens mate in one. How should you treat that threat?","options":["As a slow plan that can wait","As irrelevant if you attack a pawn","As urgent unless you have a stronger forcing solution","As an automatic draw"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Immediate mate threats must be stopped or countered with a sound forcing line.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M19-Q4","lesson":19,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why are checks called forcing moves?","options":["They gain seconds on the clock","They always win material","They cannot be answered by captures","The opponent must respond to the check legally"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A king in check restricts the opponent's legal responses.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M20-Q1","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A king is boxed in by its own pawns on the back rank. Which defender's move can be dangerous?","options":["A rook or queen check along the back rank","A harmless knight retreat","A pawn move on the other wing","A bishop moving to its starting square"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without a safe escape square, a back-rank check can deliver mate.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M20-Q2","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"How can a pawn move sometimes prevent a future back-rank mate?","options":["By blocking the queen's own file","By making a safe escape square for the king","By forcing both rooks to trade","By changing the king's color"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Creating luft (breathing room) can help a king escape a rook check.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M20-Q3","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You see a rook check on the back rank, but the king can escape to h7. What must you conclude?","options":["The check is automatically checkmate","The rook must be sacrificed","The escape square must be examined; the check may not be mate","Black must resign"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A back-rank pattern only mates if every safe response is ruled out.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M20-Q4","lesson":20,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent's rook is on your back rank. Which is the key question before worrying about material?","options":["Can you win a pawn?","Did you castle already?","How many moves are left?","Does the king have any legal defense against check?"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Assess king safety before material when a mating threat is present.","hint":"Examine each choice using the chess idea from this lesson.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M21-Q1","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook protects both its king and an attacked bishop. What tactical problem might the rook face?","options":["It may be overloaded by two defensive jobs","It becomes a queen","It gains the right to castle","It can no longer capture"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An overloaded defender may be forced to abandon one of its protected targets.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M21-Q2","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You capture the only piece guarding an enemy queen. What should you calculate next?","options":["Only the time remaining","Whether you can now safely win the queen","Whether your king can promote","Whether castling is legal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Removing the defender is useful if it creates a concrete follow-up.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M21-Q3","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your queen appears free to capture a rook, but a bishop protects it. What should you notice?","options":["The rook is automatically won","The bishop may not move","The rook is not really undefended","The bishop becomes pinned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Before capturing, check all pieces that guard the target.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M21-Q4","lesson":21,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A piece guards two mating squares and can defend only one. What should you examine?","options":["Only pawn structure","Only the opening name","Only rook value","A forcing sequence that attacks both duties"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Overloading succeeds when the defender cannot answer the combined threats.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M22-Q1","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A queen is the main defender of a king. What is 'deflection' meant to do?","options":["Pull the queen away from that defensive job","Trade every pawn first","Prevent all legal moves","Create an isolated pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A deflection tactic removes a piece from an important defensive square or duty.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M22-Q2","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook guards a pawn along an open file. How can an enemy bishop interfere?","options":["By promoting","By blocking the line between rook and pawn","By castling","By jumping the rook"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Interference works by placing a piece on the line connecting defender and target.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M22-Q3","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A sacrifice attracts an enemy piece to a vulnerable square. What is this often called?","options":["Opposition","A perpetual check","A decoy","A pawn race"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A decoy lures an enemy piece to a new square for a tactical purpose.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M22-Q4","lesson":22,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A queen has been drawn away, but you cannot attack its former target. What should you conclude?","options":["Every deflection wins","The defender is never useful","The tactic must be a fork","The idea is not sufficient without a concrete follow-up"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Motif recognition is a start; calculating the actual follow-up determines whether it works.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M23-Q1","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After an enemy bishop captures your knight, what should you look at before recapturing?","options":["Checks or captures that change the immediate situation","Only a recapture with the nearest pawn","Only castling moves","Only opening names"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An intermediate move can be stronger than immediately restoring material balance.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M23-Q2","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is an intermediate move (zwischenzug)?","options":["A repeated move in an endgame","A forcing move inserted before the expected continuation","A king move without check","A pawn that cannot move"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A zwischenzug interrupts the obvious line with a more urgent threat.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M23-Q3","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You can take back a pawn but also have a forcing check. Why calculate the check?","options":["Checks are always checkmate","It makes the pawn worth more","The opponent may have to respond before recapturing","Every check wins a rook"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checks restrict replies and can change the tactical outcome.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M23-Q4","lesson":23,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When is an intermediate move likely to fail?","options":["When a pawn is isolated","When you are playing White","When you have developed a bishop","When the opponent has a stronger reply that refutes your tactic"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Calculate both sides of a counter-tactic rather than assuming surprise makes it sound.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M24-Q1","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Two pieces cover the squares around an enemy king. What are you constructing?","options":["A mating net","An isolated pawn","An opposition","A backward pawn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A mating net restricts the king's safe escape squares and sets up checkmate.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M24-Q2","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may a queen alone have trouble checkmating a defended king in the middlegame?","options":["A queen cannot give check","The queen may lack support to cover escapes and defenders","The queen can only move forward","Every checking move is illegal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coordinated attackers are often needed to remove escape squares and defensive resources.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M24-Q3","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You find a check, but the king has two safe squares. What should you evaluate?","options":["Only queen value","Only captured pawns","How to cover the remaining escape squares","Only the move number"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checking is not enough; mating threats must remove legal ways out.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M24-Q4","lesson":24,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is most important before sacrificing a bishop to open the king?","options":["Whether sacrifices look exciting","Whether the bishop is your worst piece","Whether your pieces are pretty","Whether concrete follow-up moves justify the material given"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A sacrifice is sound only when attacks and responses support it.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M25-Q1","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You develop a knight and attack the opponent's queen in the same move. What benefit can this bring?","options":["Gaining a tempo by improving a piece while threatening the queen","Changing the timer","Forcing checkmate in every case","Making the queen unable to move"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A useful developing move with a threat can gain time in chess terms.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M25-Q2","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent develops three pieces while you move the same bishop back and forth without reason. What is the likely problem?","options":["Your bishop becomes a rook","You have lost useful developing turns","You must offer a draw","Your king has promoted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Repeated unnecessary moves can leave you behind in development.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M25-Q3","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your knight must retreat because it is attacked by a pawn. Is that always a waste of time?","options":["Yes, every retreat loses","Yes, knights cannot retreat","No; a necessary defensive move may be best","No; tempo never matters"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chess principles have exceptions when a concrete threat must be met.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M25-Q4","lesson":25,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does 'losing time' mean in opening analysis?","options":["Losing 30 seconds on the clock","Having fewer pawns","Moving second","Using moves without useful progress while your opponent improves"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Tempo describes the value of moves, not time on the clock.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M26-Q1","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In an Italian-type structure, a pawn on c3 commonly supports which central advance?","options":["d4","h4","a4","f4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The pawn on c3 protects d4, preparing White to challenge central pawns.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M26-Q2","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your central pawn push opens a file where your queen is exposed. What should you do before playing it?","options":["Trust the opening name","Calculate captures and open lines","Always push anyway","Ignore the queen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pawn breaks change lines and often create tactical threats to long-range pieces.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M26-Q3","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your pawn break trades two central pawns. What should you examine next?","options":["Whether the clocks match","How many rooks remain on the board at the start","Which pieces gain active squares from the opened center","Whether the opening is famous"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A pawn break is useful when its structural changes improve the resulting position.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M26-Q4","lesson":26,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When should you delay a pawn break?","options":["Whenever your knight moves","Only if you are Black","Whenever there are queens","When it creates immediate tactics against your own king or pieces"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Calculated dangers override the general desire to fight for central space.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M27-Q1","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6, which White third move immediately challenges e5 with a pawn?","options":["d4","h3","a3","g3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Scotch Game begins with the central pawn break 3.d4.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M27-Q2","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"White plays Bb5 after developing Nf3. Which Black piece does the bishop pressure?","options":["The queen on d8","The knight on c6","The rook on h8","The bishop on c8"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Ruy Lopez bishop puts pressure on the knight that helps guard e5.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M27-Q3","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a useful way to choose between two sound opening systems?","options":["Pick the longest opening name","Avoid learning plans","Compare their typical positions and plans","Never change openings after choosing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Learning plans and pawn structures supports informed opening choices.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M27-Q4","lesson":27,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent deviates from a prepared Scotch line. What should you do?","options":["Play the next memorized move blindly","Sacrifice your queen","Ignore the opponent's threat","Check tactics and central control before choosing a response"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Opening understanding matters more than memorizing a sequence.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M28-Q1","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In 1.d4 d5 2.c4, what is White primarily doing?","options":["Pressuring the d5 pawn and central control","Attacking h7 with a queen","Forcing immediate checkmate","Losing a bishop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Queen's Gambit begins with c4 challenging Black's central pawn.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M28-Q2","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Many London System setups develop the c1 bishop outside the pawn chain. Which square is often used?","options":["h6","f4","a7","e8"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bf4 is a common early London System development.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M28-Q3","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your London bishop is attacked by a pawn. What should you consider?","options":["Ignore it because the London is always safe","Sacrifice it automatically","Whether a safe retreat or exchange improves your position","Move your king out immediately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A system is not a fixed script; respond to the actual position.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M28-Q4","lesson":28,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is a mistake when studying the Queen's Gambit?","options":["Learning why c4 challenges d5","Considering pawn structure","Practicing different replies","Assuming you must regain any offered pawn immediately"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Queen's Gambit positions can offer long-term central compensation rather than instant recapture.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M29-Q1","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After 1.e4, which Black pawn move begins the Sicilian Defense?","options":["c5","e5","e6","c6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1...c5 begins the Sicilian, challenging the center from the side.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M29-Q2","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the French Defense, 1...e6 usually prepares what central pawn move?","options":["...h6","...d5","...a5","...g5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Black intends to challenge White's center with ...d5.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M29-Q3","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why does a Sicilian structure often produce asymmetry?","options":["Both sides must castle queenside","The kings swap sides","Black contests e4 with the c-pawn rather than matching ...e5","Both queens disappear immediately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The c-pawn challenge yields pawn structures different from symmetric king-pawn openings.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M29-Q4","lesson":29,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are choosing a Black response to e4. What should matter besides opening names?","options":["Only whether a title sounds strong","Only whether a grandmaster played it yesterday","Whether the first move attacks a rook","Whether you understand typical plans and resulting pawn structures"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Familiarity with recurring plans helps you handle deviations.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M30-Q1","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After 1.d4 d5 2.c4, which pawn move begins the Slav Defense?","options":["...c6","...g6","...h6","...b6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"...c6 supports d5 without immediately restricting Black's c8 bishop.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M30-Q2","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"In the Queen's Gambit Declined, what is a familiar way to defend d5?","options":["...h5","...e6","...g5","...a6"],"answer":1,"explanation":"...e6 supports the d5 pawn, though it can temporarily block the light-squared bishop.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M30-Q3","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"The King's Indian allows White a large pawn center. What should Black plan for?","options":["No central counterplay","Immediate queen trades only","Well-timed pawn breaks and active pieces","Leaving every piece at home"],"answer":2,"explanation":"King's Indian play often relies on challenging an established center later.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M30-Q4","lesson":30,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the key difference between copying moves and understanding an opening?","options":["The color of your pieces","The number of move labels","Whether there is an engine","Knowing the plans and pawn breaks that make moves useful"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Plans remain relevant even when the opponent leaves a known line.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M31-Q1","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can a rook benefit from an open file?","options":["It has an unobstructed path along the column","It may jump over pieces","It becomes worth nine points","It promotes on the back rank"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An open file has no pawns blocking rook movement.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M31-Q2","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your rook controls a file but cannot enter the enemy position. What might help?","options":["Move it backward forever","Create or contest an entry square","Forget all other pieces","Sacrifice the queen automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rook activity matters most when control of a file leads to useful penetration or pressure.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M31-Q3","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You plan to double rooks on a file. Which question matters?","options":["Whether you can castle twice","Whether both pawns have promoted","Whether the extra rook increases pressure on a useful target","Whether the file has a nice name"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Doubling is useful when the combined pressure serves a real goal.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M31-Q4","lesson":31,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your rook is on an open file but a bishop is hanging. What should you do first?","options":["Continue the rook plan without checking","Ignore the bishop","Assume open files guarantee victory","Respond to the immediate material threat"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Good positional ideas do not excuse blunders.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M32-Q1","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"An isolated d-pawn has no friendly pawns on c- or e-files. What could make it weak?","options":["It cannot be protected by neighboring pawns","It automatically promotes","It captures vertically","It cannot advance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An isolated pawn lacks support from adjacent friendly pawns.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M32-Q2","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might someone willingly accept an isolated queen's pawn?","options":["It makes the king immune to check","The position may provide open lines and active piece play","It guarantees an endgame win","It prevents exchanges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dynamic compensation can justify a structural weakness.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M32-Q3","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When an isolated pawn advances, what often changes?","options":["The players switch colors","The king loses castling rights automatically","Lines and squares can open for both sides","The pawn becomes a rook"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Central pawn pushes should be assessed for their effect on piece activity and threats.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M32-Q4","lesson":32,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which is too simplistic when judging a pawn weakness?","options":["Comparing defender activity","Looking for pressure on the pawn","Evaluating pawn breaks","Assuming every isolated pawn causes a forced loss"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A static weakness may be balanced by useful activity.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M33-Q1","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight reaches a central square supported by a pawn and safe from enemy pawns. What is that often called?","options":["An outpost","A skewer","A discovered check","A mating net"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A durable outpost is a strong square for a piece, often a knight, that enemy pawns cannot easily chase away.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M33-Q2","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your bishop is blocked by its own center pawns. What can improve it?","options":["Keep it trapped deliberately","Change the pawn structure or reroute the bishop","Trade the king","Advance only rook pawns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pawn structure often determines which bishop diagonals are available.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M33-Q3","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A knight looks beautifully centralized, but an enemy pawn can kick it away immediately. What is missing?","options":["A queen trade","A checkmate threat","A secure outpost","A move number"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A true outpost must be resistant to attack by opposing pawns.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M33-Q4","lesson":33,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before placing a knight on an advanced square, what should you check?","options":["Only its visual appearance","Only whether it gives check","Whether you can name its color","Whether it can be driven away or captured"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A strong piece placement depends on how the opponent can respond.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M34-Q1","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are considering sacrificing a bishop near the enemy king. What should justify it?","options":["Concrete attacking continuations and adequate compensation","The bishop's color","How famous the opening is","The number of clocks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sacrifices are sound only when calculation supports the attack or compensation.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M34-Q2","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why might a sacrifice fail even with multiple attackers?","options":["Because all attacks are illegal","The defending king may have a safe escape or tactical resource","Because bishops are never valuable","Because queens cannot defend"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Count the defender's safe replies and resources before committing material.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M34-Q3","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Your opponent can force a queen trade that ends your attack. What should you do?","options":["Sacrifice more material automatically","Ignore the trade","Reevaluate the attack before sacrificing","Move the king toward their pawns"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A forced simplification may remove the attack's compensation.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M34-Q4","lesson":34,"kind":"mc","prompt":"When is retreating preferable to a speculative sacrifice?","options":["Never","Only in endgames","Only when Stockfish is unavailable","When the best defensive line defeats your attack"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Improving the position is better than an unsound attack.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M35-Q1","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Two kings face each other across one square, and the other side must move. What concept might help?","options":["Opposition","An en passant capture","A discovered check","A fork"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Direct opposition can force a king to yield useful squares when no waiting moves remain.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M35-Q2","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What is the main benefit of an active king in an endgame?","options":["It moves like a queen","It can support pawns and restrict the enemy king","It can jump opposing pieces","It may move into check"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fewer attacking pieces make the king a valuable active defender and attacker.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M35-Q3","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why may opposition fail to produce a win?","options":["Because kings do not move in endgames","Because opposition is illegal","The pawn layout and available moves can change the result","The move numbers are too low"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Opposition does not substitute for calculating king moves and pawn breaks.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M35-Q4","lesson":35,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before advancing a passed pawn, what should you calculate?","options":["Only whether it looks advanced","Only your last opening move","Only pawn color","Whether the opposing king or another pawn can stop it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pawn races depend on accurate move counting.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M36-Q1","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A passed pawn is three moves from promotion. What is the key chase question?","options":["Can the enemy king reach its path before promotion?","Does it have the same color square as a bishop?","Did it originate from d2?","Does the defender have a rook on a1?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Count both the pawn's advances and the king's legal route.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M36-Q2","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What does the square rule approximate?","options":["Which pawn can capture diagonally","Whether a king can catch an advancing pawn","Which side castles first","Whether a rook has an open file"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The square rule is a geometric shortcut for a king's pawn chase.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M36-Q3","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can the square rule give an incomplete answer?","options":["A king can leap over pieces","All pawn moves are identical","Checks, captures and obstacles may change the race","Promotion is optional"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The shortcut assumes a straightforward chase; actual tactics can alter the result.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M36-Q4","lesson":36,"kind":"mc","prompt":"What should you check if two pawns on opposite wings race to promote?","options":["Only the king's starting square","Only which pawn moved last","Only total pawn count","Move order and whether either side can stop or check the other"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The exact sequence matters in pawn races.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M37-Q1","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"For a friendly passed pawn, where can a rook often help without blocking it?","options":["Behind the pawn","Directly ahead of it on its path","On any closed file","Off the board"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A rook behind the pawn supports its advance along the file.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M37-Q2","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Why can rook endgames be drawn even with an extra pawn?","options":["Rooks cannot capture pawns","Active checks and defensive rook placement can compensate","The extra pawn must promote instantly","Kings cannot move"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rook activity and king safety often matter as much as the pawn count.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M37-Q3","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You have a rook behind your passed pawn, but the enemy rook gives checks. What is urgent?","options":["Push regardless of king danger","Ignore the checks","Find safe king placement and check defenses","Trade your queen"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Check activity can prevent conversion of an advantage.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M37-Q4","lesson":37,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A rook occupies the open seventh rank. What should you examine?","options":["Only its point value","Only the opening name","Only pawn color","Whether it can attack enemy pawns and restrict the king"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Active rooks often target pawns from the side or behind.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M38-Q1","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"The opponent has no legal moves and is not in check. What is the result?","options":["Stalemate, a draw","Checkmate, a win","A forced pawn promotion","An illegal position in every case"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stalemate is a draw because the side to move has no legal move but is not checked.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M38-Q2","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You are ahead a queen. Why can exchanging extra pieces help?","options":["It removes all legal moves instantly","It may reduce counterplay and simplify conversion","It forces a stalemate","It makes pawns worth more"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Simplification can make a material advantage easier to convert, provided you avoid drawn endings.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M38-Q3","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You want to give a final check with a queen. What should you verify?","options":["Whether the queen moved earlier","Whether you can castle","Whether the enemy king is checked and has no legal defense","Whether you have more time on the clock"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checkmate requires no safe response to check.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M38-Q4","lesson":38,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which mistake can turn a winning king-and-queen ending into a draw?","options":["Using a rook to help","Giving check to force the king away","Keeping the queen protected","Leaving the defender with no legal moves while not in check"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Stalemate is especially easy when the defender has very few pieces.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M39-Q1","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Before turning on the engine in game review, what should you do?","options":["Write what you thought and what you missed","Copy the engine's best line immediately","Delete the moves you lost","Only count blunders"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Your own thought process is necessary for diagnosing recurring mistakes.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M39-Q2","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Stockfish finds a stronger move. What is the useful learning question?","options":["Which emoji best describes it?","Why was it stronger and how could you have noticed it?","How to hide the score","Which opponent played it first?"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The purpose of review is a reusable decision improvement, not just a number.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M39-Q3","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You repeatedly miss loose pieces in games. What kind of review takeaway helps most?","options":["Remember every move in each game","Change openings every day","Add a pre-move scan for undefended pieces","Play only bullet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A repeatable checklist translates review into an improved habit.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M39-Q4","lesson":39,"kind":"mc","prompt":"Which annotation is least useful?","options":["I missed a capture because I focused on my own threat","I should check opponent checks after every move","I developed too slowly and my king stayed in the center","I lost because I am simply bad at chess"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A helpful annotation names a specific decision and an actionable correction.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M40-Q1","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You calculated a promising capture. When should you evaluate the resulting position?","options":["After considering the opponent's strongest reply","Before checking whether the capture is legal","Only after the game ends","Only if Stockfish is open"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A move must survive a realistic opponent response to be a good candidate.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M40-Q2","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"You solved a puzzle only after several hints. How should mastery track it?","options":["As an unassisted perfect score","As assisted learning that needs a later fresh test","As a forced game loss","As an automatic promotion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hints can support learning but do not demonstrate independent mastery.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M40-Q3","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"After missing a tactic, what is the best next step?","options":["Memorize the answer without explanation","Avoid the topic forever","Identify the tactical clue you missed and practice another example","Replay only wins"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Understanding the missed pattern transfers to new positions.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"},{"id":"M40-Q4","lesson":40,"kind":"mc","prompt":"A move wins a pawn but allows a forcing checkmate against you. What matters?","options":["Material count alone","How good the move looks","Only your pawn structure","Rejecting the move because the opponent's forcing reply wins"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Calculate opponent responses before celebrating material gains.","hint":"Apply the concept from the lesson to this new situation.","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"}];
